## The short version

On Pitch Day you hear every proposal in the room. Before you leave, you rank the projects you would want to help build — and say whether you would rather go solo. Think it through on the paper worksheet in class, then type your answers in here. **You can resubmit until {{ TEAM_PREF_DUE_DAY }} night**, so a weekend of second thoughts is not just allowed, it is expected.

---

## Why this assignment exists

Choosing what to work on with other people is a skill, and it is mostly not about the idea. Teams form this way constantly — a startup deciding which of three directions to chase, an open-source project deciding which fork to back, a group inside a company signing up for the next quarter's work. The question is never only *which project is best*, it is *which project will I still care about in ten weeks, alongside these particular people, given what I actually want to learn.*

Two failure modes show up every time. The first is ranking by charisma: the most confident presenter gets the votes, and the evidence gets forgotten by the time you're voting. The second is ranking by comfort: you pick the project closest to what you already know, and spend the term not learning anything. Naming your reasons in writing is the defense against both — it forces the vote to be about something you could defend later.

*In this course:* teams are announced at the start of the next session, and your ranked choices are the primary input. The written proposals are posted, so you can spend the weekend reading rather than trusting your memory of a six-minute pitch.

---

## What to produce

The printable worksheet is handed out in class and is also posted with the session materials. It asks for three things, and your submission has the same three:

1. **Your ranked choices** — up to three anchor projects you would join, in order, with a one-line *why* for each, in the language of the selection criteria (value, richness, scope, stack, what you'd build there). The *why* matters more than the ranking.
2. **The solo option** — whether you would rather continue solo (yes, no, or open to either), and if yes, **the scope you'd cut to for one person.** Solo is a legitimate choice in this course, not a fallback, and it is graded on the same artifacts at one-person scope.
3. **What you'd bring to the team you join** — a skill, an interest, a piece of discovery you already did: something the team gains by getting you.

---

## Requirements and constraints

- **Rank every anchor you would genuinely join, up to three**, unless you are declaring solo. If three are on the list and you'd join three, rank three — ranking one is not a preference, it is an ultimatum.
- **Give a real reason for each.** "Sounds cool" tells the instructor nothing and does not help you get placed well.
- **Be honest about the solo option.** Declaring solo and being placed on a team you did not want serves nobody.
- **Edit freely until the deadline.** The written proposals are posted after Pitch Day. Read them. Changing your ranking after reading the evidence is exactly the behavior the course is teaching.

---

## Grading

Credit / no credit.

| Check | What it means |
|---|---|
| Submitted by the deadline | Your input reaches team formation |
| Ranked choices, or a solo declaration with its scope | There is a real preference to work with |
| A one-line reason per choice | The ranking is defensible, not a popularity vote |

---

## Tips and common pitfalls

- **Do not rank on the pitch alone.** The pitch is a few minutes and optimized to persuade. The written proposal is where the scope arithmetic is, and the scope arithmetic is what you will live inside.
- **A project you would enjoy but cannot contribute to is a bad first choice.** Ask what you would actually do on it in week three.
- **Not being placed first-choice is normal and is not a verdict on you.** Teams are built to be workable, and second choices routinely turn into the best experience of the term.
- **If you would genuinely rather go solo, say so plainly.** Hedging produces a placement nobody wanted.

